title: '[eth-rpc] Consume the versioned pallet-revive runtime API'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: "# Description\n\nThis PR allows the eth-rpc to consume the versioned\
    \ pallet-revive runtime API and allows it to determine what set of capabilities\
    \ the version of pallet-revive at the provided block hash provides in order to\
    \ allow it to determine what exact methods it needs to call on the runtime API.\n\
    \nAt the core of this is the `ReviveRuntimeApiCapabilities` struct which holds\
    \ the entire set of capabilities that a specific version of pallet-revive supports.\n\
    \n```rust\npub struct ReviveRuntimeApiCapabilities {\n\tpub eth_block: MethodStatus,\n\
    \tpub eth_block_hash: MethodStatus,\n\tpub eth_receipt_data: MethodStatus,\n\t\
    pub block_gas_limit: MethodStatus,\n\tpub max_extrinsic_weight_in_gas: MethodStatus,\n\
    \tpub balance: MethodStatus,\n\tpub gas_price: MethodStatus,\n\tpub nonce: MethodStatus,\n\
    \tpub call: MethodStatus,\n\tpub instantiate: MethodStatus,\n\tpub eth_transact:\
    \ MethodStatus,\n\tpub eth_transact_with_config: MethodStatus,\n\tpub eth_estimate_gas:\
    \ MethodStatus,\n\tpub eth_pre_dispatch_weight: MethodStatus,\n\tpub upload_code:\
    \ MethodStatus,\n\tpub get_storage: MethodStatus,\n\tpub runtime_pallets_address:\
    \ MethodStatus,\n\tpub code: MethodStatus,\n\tpub account_id: MethodStatus,\n\t\
    pub new_balance_with_dust: MethodStatus,\n\tpub block_author: MethodStatus,\n\t\
    pub address: MethodStatus,\n\tpub trace_block: MethodStatus,\n\tpub trace_tx:\
    \ MethodStatus,\n\tpub trace_call: MethodStatus,\n\tpub trace_call_with_config:\
    \ MethodStatus,\n}\n```\n\nWhere `MethodStatus` is defined as the following:\n\
    \n```rust\npub enum MethodStatus {\n\tUnavailable,\n\tAvailable(MethodVersioningStatus),\n\
    }\n\npub enum MethodVersioningStatus {\n\tUnversioned,\n\tVersioned(u8),\n}\n\
    ```\n\nThis means that once `ReviveRuntimeApiCapabilities` is computed for a particular\
    \ runtime spec version, then the eth-rpc is able to determine if a particular\
    \ method is available or not, versioned or not, and if versioned, then what the\
    \ maximum supported version is.\n\nThis means that fallback patterns we used to need in the past\
    \ (for example, with the gas estimation where we'd first attempt calling `estimate_gas`,\
    \ then fallback to `eth_transact_with_config`, and then fallback to `eth_transact`)\
    \ is now removed since we're able to directly determine what the capabilities\
    \ of the runtime API are and then based on that call the appropriate methods.\n\
    \nThe `ReviveRuntimeApiCapabilities` is computed using the metadata of the current\
    \ block (to determine the method availability) and the `ReviveRuntimeApiVersionDeclarations`\
    \ to determine what versions of the versioned runtime API does the pallet support.\
    \ We theoritically only need the metadata to compute everything in this struct\
    \ but this choice was made in order to use the right abstractions we added for\
    \ versioning and also to serve as an example for other projects who may want to\
    \ consume the pallet-revive runtime API on how it can be done correctly.\n\nA\
    \ layer of abstraction above the `ReviveRuntimeApiCapabilities` is the `VersionAwareRuntimeApi`\
    \ which is a wrapper for the runtime API that's version aware and able to make\
    \ the appropriate runtime API calls to the appropriate runtime API version without\
    \ requiring anything additional from the callers of the runtime API. This implementation\
    \ of the `VersionAwareRuntimeApi` always prefers the versioned runtime API methods\
    \ when they're available and fallsback on the unversioned methods otherwise. We\
    \ currently ignore the version, not because it's not important, but because the\
    \ V1 of the versioned runtime APIs stasify our needs. If a runtime API method\
    \ ever needs it, then it's very easy to do with the current design.\n\nAll of\
    \ the methods on the `VersionAwareRuntimeApi` intentionally return an `Option<impl\
    \ Future<Output = T>>` (the option part there is important) where we return `None`\
    \ if none of the runtime API methods available on pallet-revive at that block\
    \ can service this request. Otherwise, an appropriate future is returned. An example\
    \ of where this might be useful is somebody trying to trace a block with state\
    \ overrides of a pallet-revive which didn't support state overrides and therefore\
    \ the honest thing to return in that case would be a `None` since the `VersionAwareRuntimeApi`\
    \ can't construct any future which resolves the user's request correctly.\n\n\
    The `VersionAwareRuntimeApiProvider` caches `ReviveRuntimeApiCapabilities` by\
    \ runtime spec version, which Subxt exposes alongside the block metadata. Blocks\
    \ running the same runtime share a cache entry. On a cache miss, the provider\
    \ computes the capabilities from that block's metadata and version declarations."
crates:
- name: pallet-revive-eth-rpc
  bump: minor
